<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Baeza</h1><p class="st-meta">1224 CE – 1226 CE · capital Baeza</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The breakaway realm of the Almohad prince al-Bayyasi, whose alliance with Castile hastened the fall of the upper Guadalquivir.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1224, amid a succession crisis in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ii">Yusuf II</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-al-bayyasi">Abd Allah al-Bayyasi</a>, a governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, proclaimed himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baeza">Baeza</a> and sought the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> against his rivals.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-al-bayyasi">Al-Bayyasi</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baeza">Baeza</a> and nearby towns as a Castilian ally and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, handing over castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martos">Martos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andujar">Andújar</a> in exchange for military help. This open dependence on a Christian king discredited him among Andalusi Muslims.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1226 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-al-bayyasi">al-Bayyasi</a> was murdered by his own followers near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almodovar-del-rio">Almodóvar</a> while fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baeza">Baeza</a> soon passed to Ferdinand III, among the first major towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> to fall to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1224 CE – 1226 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.06%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1224 CE – 1226 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Baeza</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abd Allah al-Bayyasi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Baeza of al-Bayyasi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1225&amp;focus=taifa-of-baeza" aria-label="Taifa of Baeza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4594 812 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taifa of Baeza lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4914 1068L4794 1282M4914 1068L4885 950"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4794 1282h0M4885 950h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4914 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4914 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1225&amp;focus=taifa-of-baeza">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
